<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Paired backup, on a schedule (TCRN-CROSS-STORY-128).

  WHY A TEMPLATE AND NOT A LOADED AGENT. This file is committed; loading it is a
  separate act on one operator's machine. The path below is that machine's, so a
  reader on another one must edit it before `launchctl load`. Keeping the template
  in the repository and the loading out of it is deliberate: a persistent local
  configuration is the operator's to install, and a repository that silently
  scheduled work on whoever checked it out would be doing something nobody asked
  for.

  WHY --if-due AND NOT A PLAIN BACKUP. The flow judges each chain separately and
  skips the ones whose off-site copy is still fresh. "Not due" is the scheduler's
  normal answer, not a failure — see the runbook. It also means this agent can run
  often and back up rarely, which is what makes a laptop schedule workable: the
  machine is not always awake at any fixed hour, so the question has to be "is it
  due" rather than "is it 03:00".

  WHY 6 HOURS. The interval it enforces is 24h (AOS_BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS) with a
  6h grace before the due alert fires. Waking four times a day means a laptop that
  missed one window still backs up inside the grace, instead of the alert going
  red because the machine happened to be asleep at the appointed minute.

  PATH (TCRN-CROSS-INC-032). This ran `/bin/sh -lc … pnpm …` and every fire since it
  was loaded died at exit 127: under launchd a login `sh` does NOT source ~/.zshrc,
  which is what puts the nvm-managed pnpm on PATH. Reproduce the root cause with
  `env -i /bin/sh -lc 'command -v pnpm'` — it prints nothing. PATH is therefore stated
  explicitly below, and the nvm entry is version-pinned to match package.json's
  engines.node (24.16.0); if that pin moves, this moves. `pnpm scheduler:health` now
  reads the last exit status of every com.tcrn.* agent, because nothing did — which is
  how a 127 ran unseen for a day on three agents at once.

  RunAtLoad is false on purpose: loading an agent should not start a network
  transfer at the moment the operator is setting it up.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.tcrn.aos.paired-backup</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/sh</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>export PATH="$HOME/.nvm/versions/node/v24.16.0/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"; cd "$HOME/Code/TCRN Platform/TCRN-AOS" &amp;&amp; pnpm paired:backup-if-due</string>
  </array>

  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>21600</integer>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/tcrn-aos-paired-backup.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/tcrn-aos-paired-backup.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
